 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The worker uses a 2Q replacement policy so that the one-use sheets of a long screening do not displace the comparison working set. The cache is split into two FIFO queues:
+
+- **A1in (probationary)** holds sheets admitted and not yet referenced a second time.
+- **Am (hot)** holds sheets referenced at least twice.
+
+A1in is capped at a configurable share of the cache budget (default 25%, about 3 GiB); Am may occupy the remainder. The split is a per-production tuning parameter.
+
+On a cache hit, a sheet found in A1in is promoted to Am — a second reference marks it reused — and a sheet already in Am is moved to Am's most-recent position.
+
+On admitting a completed sheet, the sheet enters at A1in's most-recent position. If admission would exceed 12 GiB, the worker evicts from A1in's least-recent position; when A1in holds less than its cap, it evicts from Am's least-recent position instead.
+
+Because a screening scan churns A1in, its one-use sheets are evicted there and the reused sheets held in Am survive the scan. The policy metadata, including both queues, stays private to the worker and does not change the external interface, render-queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q: two FIFO queues (A1in probationary, Am hot) with a size split | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging; plain FIFO | LRU has the least bookkeeping and follows recent work, but a long sequential screening evicts the comparison sheets. Frequency-with-aging retains repeatedly popular clips, but counter maintenance and aging add work and keep stale favorites around across a project switch. 2Q protects reused sheets from one-off screening scans at the cost of a second queue and a tuning parameter. |
+| A1in size split | 25% of the 12 GiB budget, tunable | Fixed even split; fixed 10% | A small probationary queue absorbs screening scans while leaving most of the budget for the comparison working set; the exact share depends on a production's screening-to-comparison mix, so it is a tuning parameter. |
